# A Unit scrutinee on the v2 JS codegen

`BUGS.md` `js-v2-unit-pattern-does-not-match-and-unit-literal-pattern-crashes`. Two defects, one
scrutinee. The unit value is `null` on the v2 JS lane, and the emitted matcher read `.t` off it
unconditionally, so `case () =>` raised
`TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 't')` — **a pattern match crashing on a value it
simply does not match.** The same unguarded read was in both "no arm matched" error messages, so the
diagnostic for a failed match crashed too, precisely where a diagnostic was wanted.

Separately, `$isTag` began `!!(v && …)`, and `null` is falsy, so `case _: Unit` could never hold.
Same shape as the interpreter and the v1 JS backend, both fixed earlier the same day: a type-test
table listing every type *except* this one.

```scalascript
def viaAscription(x: Any): String = x match
  case _: Unit => "UNIT"
  case _ => "OTHER"

def viaLiteral(x: Any): String = x match
  case () => "UNIT"
  case _ => "OTHER"

println("ascription-unit: " + viaAscription(println("")))
val n = viaLiteral(println("")).length
println("literal-survived " + (n > 0))
println("ascription-int:  " + viaAscription(5))
println("literal-int:     " + viaLiteral(5))
```

**The `case ()` ANSWER is deliberately not pinned here — only that it does not crash.** It is
still a live cross-lane divergence, and the lanes moved under this case once already: it was filed
as two-against-two, then `dc4cf56b5` fixed literal patterns on `int`, and my frozen `OTHER` became
the corpus regression. Measured now:

| lane | `case ()` on the unit value |
|---|---|
| `jvm` (real Scala) · `js` · `int` | `UNIT` — the right answer |
| native / v2 · v2 JS | `OTHER` |

Pinning either answer makes this case red on lanes it was never about. So the unit-literal match is
CALLED — which is what would crash — and the line after it is what the golden checks. A crash aborts
before `literal-survived` prints, on every lane, without anyone having to agree about `UNIT`.
`unit-literal-pattern-diverges-two-lanes-against-two` owns the answer.

The result is USED (`.length > 0`, true for both `UNIT` and `OTHER`) rather than bound to an unused
`val`. That is not style: bound to an unused `val`, the whole call — **and the `println` side effect
inside it** — is elided on the JS lane, so the crash this case exists to catch would never be
reached there. Found while writing this line; filed as `js-unused-val-drops-side-effecting-call`.

This case pins: **no crash, and agreement with the lane the v2 JS codegen is supposed to mirror.**

The two non-unit lines are the guard that the fix did not make everything match: a `null`-safe read
must still let an ordinary scrutinee reach the ordinary arms.
